{
  "fixture": "gdpdu-data-carrier",
  "description": "F-IO-012: the Z3 DATA CARRIER — flat files plus the index.xml that describes them, written to the Beschreibungsstandard für die Datenträgerüberlassung 1.6 (2019-03-01), DTD gdpdu-01-03-2019.dtd. THIS IS THE OTHER HALF OF `journalExport`, and the difference is worth knowing: journalExport produces the self-describing data SET (JSON streams + field catalogue + content hashes), which is what machine evaluability under GoBD Z3 requires; what a German auditor actually receives on the medium is flat files plus index.xml, which is what the audit software IDEA imports. datenformat.md always said this export is `a mapping, not an invention`, and until 2026-08-28 the mapping was simply not in the package — docs/gobd-conformance.md §10 carried it as its last open row and the root CLAUDE.md listed it as out of scope. WHY THE WHOLE index.xml IS PINNED HERE rather than a few fields: these are the exact bytes an auditor receives, both languages must produce them identically, and the DTD fixes ELEMENT ORDER — `Table` is (URL, Name?, Description?, Validity?, codepage?, (DecimalSymbol, DigitGroupingSymbol)?, …) and an importer rejects the file if they are shuffled. A subset expectation would let a reordering pass. Initial DTD conformance was verified on 2026-08-28 by validating this output against the published DTD with a negative control (a deliberately reordered Table is rejected); what this fixture then guards is that it stays that way. THE JOURNAL IS FLATTENED to one row per posting line because a CSV cannot nest and an auditor's first act is to sum debit and credit per account — and the text carries a semicolon and a double quote on purpose, because the delimiter and the encapsulator are exactly what a naive writer gets wrong.",
